# Container image for apps/api (the Fastify server).
#
# ## The base image is pinned by digest
#
# A tag is repointed every time upstream rebuilds it, so two builds of this file at different times
# would not be the same build. The digest is the multi-arch OCI index for the tag (linux/amd64,
# linux/arm64/v8, linux/s390x). To refresh it (no Docker daemon required, and it does not pull):
#
#     TOKEN=$(curl -s 'https://auth.docker.io/token?service=registry.docker.io&scope=repository:library/node:pull' \
#       | node -e "let d='';process.stdin.on('data',c=>d+=c).on('end',()=>process.stdout.write(JSON.parse(d).token))")
#     curl -sI -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
#       -H 'Accept: application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json' \
#       -H 'Accept: application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json' \
#       https://registry-1.docker.io/v2/library/node/manifests/24.18.0-alpine3.24 \
#       | grep -i docker-content-digest
#
# With the daemon: `docker buildx imagetools inspect node:24.18.0-alpine3.24`. Keep the tag in the
# reference beside the digest, so a reader can see the version without resolving the hash.
#
# ## Why Alpine
#
# The runtime stage descends from `base`, so the base's OS packages are the shipped images' whole OS
# vulnerability surface. trivy 0.72.0 (HIGH/CRITICAL, base images alone, arm64): bookworm-slim 30,
# trixie-slim 51 with 36 fixable (blocking under container-scan.mjs's block-when-fixed policy, since
# no stage runs apt-get), alpine3.24 0. Distroless does not fit: both runtime stages need a shell for
# `RUN rm -rf`, it puts node at /nodejs/bin/node (breaking `checkArtefact` in
# scripts/release-artifacts.mjs), and it has no `node` user for `USER node`, the CI `Config.User`
# assertion and the uid=1000 tmpfs in docker-compose.staging.yml.
#
# What Alpine requires of this repository:
#
#   * No native compilation: `ignoreScripts: true` and `onlyBuiltDependencies: []` in
#     pnpm-workspace.yaml, plus `--ignore-scripts` on every install, so node-gyp never runs.
#   * pnpm-lock.yaml carries the musl variants (`@next/swc-linux-*-musl`, `@img/sharp-linuxmusl-*`,
#     `@biomejs/cli-linux-*-musl`) and no `supportedArchitectures` block pins a libc.
#   * No bash: `/bin/sh` is busybox ash, so every `RUN` stays POSIX (no `[[ ]]`, arrays or
#     `pipefail`). busybox `find` does support `-mindepth`/`-maxdepth`/`-exec … +`.
#   * `apk` stays in the image: deleting it removes the apk database trivy reads to identify the OS,
#     and container-scan.mjs fails a scan whose OS it cannot identify. The read-only root and uid 1000
#     mean it cannot install anything at runtime.
#   * musl is not glibc: thread stack sizes (the `resourceLimits` Workers in
#     apps/api/src/workers/pool.ts) and DNS resolution (the Postgres client connects by hostname)
#     differ. A zero scan means "no known-vulnerable OS package", not "safe".
#
# ## No `# syntax=` directive
#
# One would make every build download a frontend image from a mutable tag first, and nothing here
# needs a feature outside the built-in frontend.
#
# ## Stage layout
#
#   base       bare pinned image. The runtime stage descends from this, so pnpm
#              and corepack shims never reach the shipped image.
#   toolchain  base + corepack. Every build stage descends from this.
#   source     toolchain + the whole build context.
#   prod-deps  a `--prod` install, then the sources pruned away. Runtime
#              dependencies and manifests only.
#   build      a full install (typescript and @types are devDependencies), then
#              `tsc`.
#   runtime    prod-deps' /app, with the compiled dist directories laid over it.
#
# Two installs, not install-build-prune: pnpm 11 answers `pnpm install --prod` over a full install by
# purging node_modules, which without a TTY aborts with ERR_PNPM_ABORTED_REMOVE_MODULES_DIR_NO_TTY and
# with `CI=true` silently re-downloads the tree. BuildKit runs the two installs in parallel.
#
# `COPY . .`, not each package.json by name: `verifyDepsBeforeRun: error` (pnpm-workspace.yaml) fails
# every build with ERR_PNPM_VERIFY_DEPS_BEFORE_RUN the day a `packages/*` is added and the list is one
# short. The cost is ~12 seconds of install on every source change.
#
# ## Why the runtime stage copies prod-deps' whole /app
#
# `nodeLinker: isolated` makes node_modules a farm of relative symlinks into `node_modules/.pnpm`,
# which resolve only if the tree is reproduced at the same path: hence /app in every stage and one
# whole-tree copy. Cherry-picked package directories fail open: a new runtime dependency ships an image
# that cannot resolve it.
#
# Besides node_modules that tree holds the workspace package.json files (Node needs them for
# `"type": "module"` and the `exports` maps), the lockfile, pnpm-workspace.yaml and .npmrc. No
# secrets: .npmrc holds only registry URLs, and .dockerignore excludes `.env` and `.env.*` at every
# directory depth.
#
# ## What is deliberately NOT in the image
#
# TypeScript sources, tests, every tsconfig, all of apps/web except its package.json, and
# `apps/api/scripts/`, removed by the prune in prod-deps. The prune is mostly a deny list, so its worst
# failure is a fat image rather than a broken one, and a fat image is not a red build.
#
# Migrations therefore run from the host (`pnpm run db:migrate`). Running them in-cluster would mean
# adding `COPY --from=build /app/apps/api/scripts /app/apps/api/scripts` to the runtime stage, which
# gives the serving container schema-change rights.
#
# `db/migrations` itself IS in the image, as read-only data for the boot-time schema guard; see that
# COPY line.
#
# ## Signals
#
# No dumb-init or tini: `docker run --init` / `init: true` in docker-compose.yml supplies Docker's own
# PID 1, which reaps zombies and forwards SIGTERM.

FROM node:24.18.0-alpine3.24@sha256:a0b9bf06e4e6193cf7a0f58816cc935ff8c2a908f81e6f1a95432d679c54fbfd AS base
WORKDIR /app

# Pinned ahead of the base, whose digest still ships the vulnerable versions: CVE-2026-14456 (HIGH,
# libcrypto3/libssl3 3.5.7-r0), CVE-2026-107891 (musl 1.2.6-r2) and CVE-2026-85091 (zlib 1.3.2-r0).
# Exact versions rather than a bare `apk upgrade`, so two builds of one commit match (the property
# release-artifacts.mjs protects) and a version Alpine drops fails the build by name instead of
# floating. In `base`, so every stage carries it; the read-only uid-1000 runtime cannot undo it.
RUN apk add --no-cache --upgrade libcrypto3=3.5.9-r0 libssl3=3.5.9-r0 \
      musl=1.2.6-r3 musl-utils=1.2.6-r3 zlib=1.4-r0

FROM base AS toolchain
# corepack activates the exact pnpm named in the root package.json's
# `packageManager` field, and verifies it against the sha256 recorded there. That
# is the whole reason this is corepack and not `npm i -g pnpm`.
ENV COREPACK_ENABLE_DOWNLOAD_PROMPT=0 \
    NPM_CONFIG_UPDATE_NOTIFIER=false
RUN corepack enable

# .dockerignore keeps node_modules, dist, .next, coverage, .git, deploy, docs, markdown and .env*
# out of the context at every depth, so this is source plus manifests and nothing sensitive.
FROM toolchain AS source
COPY . .

# `--filter "@prl/api..."` is @prl/api and everything it depends on, which excludes apps/web and
# packages/ui: no Next or React in this image. `--ignore-scripts` repeats pnpm-workspace.yaml's
# `ignoreScripts: true` so the build does not depend on that file continuing to say so.
FROM source AS prod-deps
RUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --prod --ignore-scripts --filter "@prl/api..."
# Everything the runtime does not execute. A deny list, so its worst case is a fat image rather than
# a missing module: every package.json stays (`"type": "module"`, `exports`), and
# packages/content/data stays because the compiled content package reads its JSON from disk.
#
#   * The markdown lines repeat .dockerignore inside the build, so a change to that file cannot put
#     agent instructions (apps/web/AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md) back into a layer.
#   * `tsconfig*.json`: `tsconfig.json` does not match `tsconfig.test.json`.
#   * apps/web is an allow list (keep package.json, delete the rest): this image executes nothing
#     from it and needs only the manifest to resolve the workspace, so a new root-level file there
#     cannot ship.
#   * `docker-compose*.yml`, `.gitignore` and `.dockerignore` map the deployment's topology and
#     secret paths, and nothing at runtime reads them. release-artifacts.mjs's image probe looks only
#     for `*.md` and `.env*`, so nothing else would notice them here.
RUN rm -rf \
      apps/*/src apps/*/test apps/*/scripts apps/*/public \
      packages/*/src packages/*/test \
      apps/*/tsconfig*.json packages/*/tsconfig*.json tsconfig.base.json \
      biome.json db .github scripts Dockerfile.api Dockerfile.web \
      docker-compose*.yml .dockerignore .gitignore deploy \
      ./*.md apps/*/*.md packages/*/*.md \
 && find apps/web -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 ! -name package.json -exec rm -rf {} +

# NODE_ENV is deliberately NOT production in this stage: pnpm treats NODE_ENV=production as an
# implicit `--prod`, which omits typescript and @types/node and fails the build with
# "cannot find name 'process'".
FROM source AS build
RUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --ignore-scripts --filter "@prl/api..."
# One recursive, topologically-ordered build: each workspace dependency emits its
# dist/*.d.ts before the package that consumes it is compiled. Filtering to
# "@prl/api..." rather than running `build:packages` matters — the latter includes
# packages/ui, whose typecheck needs the React types that this filtered install
# deliberately left out.
RUN pnpm --filter "@prl/api..." run build
# Reduce this stage to what the runtime lays over prod-deps: every package's dist/ (and
# packages/content/data), and apps/api/dist. node_modules MUST go: this is a full install, and copying
# it over prod-deps' would reinstate typescript and every @types package in the shipped image.
# Deleting here and copying whole directories below means a new workspace dependency is included
# automatically; a per-package COPY list fails open, with ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND at boot.
# `tsconfig*.json` because /app/packages is copied from here into the runtime.
RUN rm -rf \
      packages/*/node_modules packages/*/src packages/*/test packages/*/tsconfig*.json \
      apps/api/node_modules apps/api/src apps/api/test apps/api/scripts apps/api/tsconfig*.json

FROM base AS runtime

# NODE_ENV=production only here, where nothing installs. PORT is the contract with
# docker-compose.yml and the healthcheck below.
#
# HOST=0.0.0.0 because config.ts defaults HOST to 127.0.0.1, and a process listening on loopback
# inside its own network namespace is unreachable from the compose network and from a published
# port. Set in the image so `docker run` is correct too (it mirrors HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0 in
# Dockerfile.web). It is safe because the network namespace, not the bind address, is the isolation
# boundary. A sidecar sharing a namespace can set HOST back to 127.0.0.1; the healthcheck stays honest.
ENV NODE_ENV=production \
    PORT=3001 \
    HOST=0.0.0.0

# Remove npm, corepack and yarn from the RUNTIME image. Nothing here runs a package manager (the
# command is `node apps/api/dist/server.js`), and one is a downloader and arbitrary-code executor
# beside the application in an image that runs with a read-only root to rule out both.
#
# The Alpine base ships yarn 1.22.22 at /opt/yarn-v1.22.22. This is a deny list, so re-run
# `ls /usr/local/bin /opt` whenever the base changes. The /usr/local/bin entries are symlinks into
# those directories, removed by name so none is left dangling. Before `USER node`, because /usr/local
# and /opt are root-owned.
RUN rm -rf /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm /usr/local/lib/node_modules/corepack \
      /usr/local/bin/npm /usr/local/bin/npx /usr/local/bin/corepack \
      /opt/yarn-v1.22.22 /usr/local/bin/yarn /usr/local/bin/yarnpkg

COPY --from=prod-deps --chown=node:node /app /app
# Two directory-wide copies, not a per-package list. COPY merges into an existing directory, so
# prod-deps' node_modules symlink farm survives underneath and the compiled output lands on top.
# packages/content/data comes along because tsc will not emit a file from above rootDir and the
# compiled content package reads it at runtime; without it every content route 500s.
COPY --from=build --chown=node:node /app/packages /app/packages
COPY --from=build --chown=node:node /app/apps/api/dist /app/apps/api/dist
# The migration FILES, not the runner: apps/api/src/schema-guard.ts compares them with
# prl.schema_migrations at boot and refuses to serve a schema behind this build. This path is the one
# the guard resolves from dist/ (three levels up, as from src/). No schema-change rights follow: no
# apps/api/scripts, and DATABASE_URL names a member of prl_app, which holds no CREATE. No --chown: the
# process reads these and has no business being able to rewrite them.
COPY --from=source /app/db/migrations /app/db/migrations

# uid/gid 1000, present in the official Node images. Never root: a process that
# cannot write to its own code cannot be persuaded to modify it.
USER node

EXPOSE 3001

# `node -e` rather than wget: node is the one binary a Node image is guaranteed to have, and the exec
# form keeps a shell out of the probe (a wget probe would need `CMD-SHELL` to interpolate the hostname).
#
# The probe targets `os.hostname()`, NOT 127.0.0.1. Docker resolves a container's own hostname to its
# address on the container network, so a server bound to loopback fails the probe instead of
# reporting healthy while unreachable from everywhere.
#
# start-period covers migration waits and connection-pool warm-up; the interval is unhurried because a
# healthcheck that polls hard is a load generator pointed at your own service.
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --start-period=30s --retries=3 \
  CMD ["node", "-e", "fetch(`http://${require('node:os').hostname()}:${process.env.PORT ?? 3001}/api/v1/health`).then((r)=>process.exit(r.ok?0:1)).catch(()=>process.exit(1))"]

CMD ["node", "apps/api/dist/server.js"]
